<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Christian I of Denmark</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1426">1426</a>–<a href="/years/1481">1481</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-oldenburg">House of Oldenburg</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Christian I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> was the first king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-oldenburg">House of Oldenburg</a>, elected in Denmark in 1448 and later king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> and, briefly, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>. In 1460 the estates of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schleswig">Schleswig</a> and Holstein elected him their ruler under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-ribe">Treaty of Ribe</a>, promising the lands would stay forever undivided. He founded the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copenhagen">Copenhagen</a> in 1479.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/christian-i-of-denmark.jpg" alt="Christian I of Denmark" width="320" height="410" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown artist · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Christian_I_of_Denmark,_Norway_%26_Sweden_1440s.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Christian was born in February 1426 in Oldenburg in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, the eldest son of Count Dietrich of Oldenburg and his second wife, Hedvig of Holstein. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-oldenburg">House of Oldenburg</a> was a comital family established since the 12th century west of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weser">River Weser</a>, which had gradually extended its rule over the neighbouring Frisian tribes. When Dietrich died in 1440 Christian and his brothers jointly succeeded him as counts of Oldenburg and Delmenhorst. Christian was raised by his childless uncle Adolphus, who wished him to be his heir and had him elected his successor in the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schleswig">Schleswig</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>In January 1448 King Christopher of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> died without heirs. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rigsradet">Council of the Realm</a> offered the Danish throne first to Duke Adolphus, who declined and recommended Christian. Christian had to promise to obey the Constitutio Valdemariana, which barred one person from ruling both <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schleswig">Schleswig</a> and Denmark, and to marry the dowager queen Dorothea of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a>. He was elected at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vyborg">Viborg</a> on 1 September 1448 and crowned at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copenhagen">Copenhagen</a> on 28 October 1449, when his marriage was also held.</p><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rigsradet">Council of the Realm</a> was divided between Christian and Karl Knutsson, whom <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> had elected in June 1448. In 1450 the Swedish Council forced Karl to give up his Norwegian claim, and Christian was crowned at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trondheim">Trondheim</a> on 2 August. A treaty signed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bergen">Bergen</a> on 29 August made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> and Norway elective monarchies sharing one king in perpetuity. Karl was driven into exile in 1457, and Christian was elected king of Sweden, restoring the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalmar-union">Kalmar Union</a> for a short time. His reign there ended in 1464, and his attempt to regain Sweden ended in defeat by the regent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sten-sture-the-elder">Sten Sture the Elder</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-brunkeberg">Battle of Brunkeberg</a> in October 1471.</p><p>In 1460, after Adolphus's death, Christian became Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schleswig">Schleswig</a> and Count of Holstein-Rendsburg, his succession confirmed by the estates at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ribe">Ribe</a> on 5 March 1460. Emperor Frederick III raised him to Duke of Holstein in 1474. Many parts of his realm sought to govern themselves, and there were constant struggles. With the permission of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-sixtus-iv">Pope Sixtus IV</a> given in 1475, he inaugurated the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copenhagen">Copenhagen</a> on 1 June 1479.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Christian died at Copenhagen Castle on 21 May 1481, aged 55, and was buried at Roskilde Cathedral in the Chapel of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magi">Magi</a>. The dynasty he founded went on to rule <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>, and it held the throne of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> until 1818 and again from 1905.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Christian I of Denmark” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>On a ridge outside Stockholm, the Swedish regent Sten Sture the Elder, with peasants, miners and burghers, defeated King <b>Christian I of Denmark</b>, who sought to enforce his rule over Sweden under the Kalmar Union.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/october-10">10 October in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The Rendsburg line died out in 1459, and the estates of Schleswig and Holstein chose <b>King Christian I of Denmark</b> as ruler in 1460 under the Treaty of Ribe.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-holstein">County of Holstein</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1448 Count <b>Christian of Oldenburg</b> was elected King of Denmark as Christian I, founding the House of Oldenburg, which later ruled Denmark, Norway, Sweden, Russia and Greece in its various branches.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-oldenburg">County of Oldenburg</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1460 the estates of the Duchy of Schleswig and the County of Holstein elected <b>King Christian I of Denmark</b> as their ruler, promising that the lands would remain 'forever undivided'.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-holstein">Duchy of Holstein</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Oldenburg, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in Copenhagen Castle, in what is now <a href="/countries/denmark">Denmark</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=53.14,8.21&amp;year=1461" aria-label="Oldenburg on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4733 334 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Christian I of Denmark"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5183 604h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5183 604h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=53.14,8.21&amp;year=1461">What was here in 1461? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
